# Former Gods, Part 2
Tamil title: மாஜி கடவுள்கள் - பகுதி 2

...the divine maidens who arose were called the Hesperides. The Greeks worshipped them too.

### Hygieia

The Greeks worshipped Hygieia as goddess of health. She was portrayed as a maiden holding a cup from which a serpent drank.

### Hymen

Hymen, son of Apollo, was the Greek god of marriage.

### Aesculapius

Apollo’s son Aesculapius was god of medicine, holding a staff encircled by a serpent.

### Pomona

Romans worshipped Pomona so that fruit might flourish in their gardens.

### Proteus

Proteus was a Greek sea-god able to foretell the future. Because he suddenly changed form, his prophecy succeeded only when spoken in his natural shape.

### Ra

Ra was Egypt’s important sun-god.

### Rhea

Rhea was mother of the Greek gods Zeus, Pluto, Hera, and others.

### Venus

Romans worshipped Venus as goddess of love.

### Vesta

Romans worshipped Vesta as goddess of the household, with a place of worship in every home.

### Vulcan

Vulcan was Rome’s god of fire and made thunderbolts for Jupiter.

### The Goddess of Crete

Crete possessed a serpent-maiden deity.

### Zeus

Zeus was the Greeks’ primal god, enthroned in heaven bearing the thunderbolt. Born to Cronus and Rhea, he revolted against his father with his siblings, seized authority, distributed realms among them, married his own sister, and supposedly protected the world.

Countless gods once occupied temples in these lands. Though poets comparable to Kalidasa and Kamban praised them, all are now former gods. People then believed they possessed many weapons and miraculous powers, and mythologies strengthened that belief as sacred stories. Thinkers courageously separated religion from tales created while minds were clouded. They endured beatings, torture, burning, and murder because people regarded mythologists as inspired poets and priests as divine messengers. Their service made possible thousands of institutions of knowledge and human advancement.

Calling long-worshipped deities phantoms was neither easy nor work for everyone. It required extraordinary courage against crowds riding the wild horse of ignorance and bearing cruelty’s spear. Opposing rulers risked life; opposing entrenched gods, stories, festivals, rituals, and the priesthoods living from them was harder still.

Spreading new knowledge resembled standing alone outside a fortified stronghold against its armed occupant. Yet because some accepted that danger, the flat world became round, many-headed and sacrifice-demanding gods receded, and the truth of one formless God began to shine.

The preceding catalogue appeared under the editorial heading `தழகநிலை` and formed Part 1 of the serial “Former Gods.”

Zeus defeated his father, took his throne, and ruled on his mother’s counsel. He gave Cronus a powerful substance that caused him to disgorge alive Poseidon, Pluto, Hestia, Demeter, Hera, and even the stone swallowed in place of a child.

## Zeus

Those who consider themselves defenders of theism may rage at the title: “Can beginningless God be former?” Look again. The title is not “Former God,” but “Former Gods.”

Everywhere there was once an age of polytheism: father-god, mother-god, rival wife-god, son-god, daughter-god, gods for day and night, famine and relief, war and thunder. Before the truths that God is one and morality is religion gained authority, people worshipped innumerable deities.

When reason dawned, people recognized that polytheism was neither true theism nor religion but an invention through which cunning priests controlled deluded minds. Many gods thereby became former gods. We introduce them to people who mistake cleansing religion of contamination for atheism.

These former gods once sat in towered temples amid priests and devotees, praised by poets and enriched by rulers. Inspired poets wrote great puranas of their grace and power. Today their images stand not in resounding temples but museums, for researchers to photograph and students to compare history and ideas. In lands that once exalted them, monotheism now prevails.

Among them was Zeus, once the Greeks’ supreme and primal god. Today the educated call his power stories yarns jointly fashioned by priest and poet, but before retirement his prestige was immeasurable. Romans worshipped the same deity as Jupiter.

Their land had poets capable of garlanding his “sacred virtues.” Homer, one of the world’s great poets, portrayed him as all-seeing, enthroned upon refined gold, ruler beneath whose nod gods trembled and fate itself moved. Today this demonstrates poetic imagination, not devotion to Zeus.

Imagine his immense temple, towering statue, halls, tanks, gardens, walls, kitchens, rulers and nobles bowing, a stern priest before heaps of offerings. His worship likely praised him:

“Praise, creator of the world!
Praise Zeus, lord of immortals!
Praise, destroyer of demons!
Grant sacred grace!
Praise, bearer of thunder,
king of this world and the next,
sender of rain and storm,
destroyer of clouds,
protector of earth!”

It could also praise conduct contrary even to ordinary humanity: “Praise, conqueror of your father! Praise, husband of your sister!” His mythology celebrated both, just as worship here praises Murugan for instructing his father and marrying the tribal maiden. Today even children cannot accept such lines. Greece has neither their expert priests nor their temples; Zeus himself is absent, a former god.

Romans similarly worshipped Jupiter with four white horses, white-clad priests, and white animal sacrifices. He too vanished from public religion after minds brightened.

Myth placed Zeus upon Mount Olympus, majestic, long-haired, sometimes in goatskin and sometimes flowing silk, with an eagle beside him like Nandi beside Siva. Belief in every deed attributed to him was theism; doubt was atheism punishable by torture.

Unlike today’s concept of beginningless God, Zeus had parents and grandparents involved in enmity and war. Questioning this could cost one’s tongue. Hear, then, the birth and upbringing of a god.

Before the world, when all was darkness, there supposedly existed Chaos and the goddess of Darkness without definite form. Asking who could have seen them when sky, earth, sea, and observers did not exist was atheism. Even in our scientific age, ask who saw thousand-headed Adisesha beneath the cosmos and religious anger erupts. Why marvel that ancient Greeks behaved similarly?

After ages, Chaos and Darkness called their son Erebus to assist them. His first great act was to expel his father and marry his mother. They produced Aether and Hemera. These children expelled their parents and ruled, and Eros was born. Together the three created sea and earth.

Earth was lifeless until Eros shot his marvellous arrow, whereupon plants grew, birds flew, fish swam, and animals roamed. Earth herself, the goddess Gaia, awoke, created Uranus from love, and married him: earth and heaven wed.

They had twelve powerful children, six male and six female. Fearing them, Uranus chained them in the underworld. Grieving Gaia urged them to attack their father. The youngest, Cronus, accepted her axe, defeated Uranus, and took the throne. Uranus cursed him to perish through his own son.

Cronus freed his siblings, married his sister Rhea, and ruled from Olympus. Whenever a child was born, fear of the curse made him swallow it. Rhea resolved to save one. When Zeus was born, she hid him with divine maidens on Mount Ida and gave Cronus a swaddled stone, which he swallowed.

The goat Amalthea nursed Zeus and supposedly attained heaven for doing so, much as our mythology grants liberation to many animals. Greeks now laugh at the goat’s salvation, while here mockery of an iguana attaining liberation still provokes cries of “atheist.”

Priests and armed attendants created clamour so Cronus would not hear the child’s cries. When he eventually learned Zeus lived and prepared to kill him, the son attacked first and defeated his father. Zeus took the throne and made Cronus disgorge the swallowed children and stone alive.

After further victories over demons and rival gods, the cosmos submitted to Zeus. Greeks worshipped him through such stories for ages without asking whether a god should have a genealogy, family war, weapons, conquest, and a kingdom. Doubt about Olympus was treated as degradation.

Today Greeks deny that God can possess such conduct. Fear and wonder before unexplained nature generated these fantasies. Sound, light, storms, rain, mountains, and sea terrified early people; they imagined many gods needing appeasement. Storytellers organized the fantasies and priests made them sacred, building temples and divine crowds.

Rationalists suffered enormously to replace ignorance with knowledge. Many whispered doubts only to be denounced as mad or possessed; others spoke openly and were tortured. Only after much blood reached sacrificial altars did reason enter public hearing. Survivors faced hostility and the gallows, yet spoke, laboured, debated, fought, and won. Zeus became a former god. Knowledge prevailed and ignorance receded.

---NOTES---
Part 2 begins as the continuation of the deity catalogue in `maji_kadavulgal1.md`; the leading ellipsis marks the inherited Hesperides entry. The Zeus article ends cleanly. `maji_kadavulgal3.md` begins a new Hera article rather than continuing a broken sentence.

The Latin-script names in parentheses are heavily corrupted by OCR. Conventional identifications are used where the Tamil name is clear: Hesperides, Hygieia, Hymen, Aesculapius, Pomona, Proteus, Ra, Rhea, Venus, Vesta, Vulcan, Zeus, Jupiter, Cronus, Hestia, Demeter, Chaos, Erebus, Aether, Hemera, Eros, Gaia, Uranus, and Amalthea. The source remains verbatim.

The editorial-heading OCR `தழகநிலை` and the associated issue marker `(314)` are uncertain and retained without speculative correction in the Tamil block.

The English translation describes Cronus’s forced disgorging conservatively because the Tamil word `கடும் விஷம்` literally suggests a potent poison, while standard retellings often describe an emetic.
